/* v4 learner-facing rewrites: The Story (10/10) */
Object.assign(window.CANON_V4_MASTERY||(window.CANON_V4_MASTERY={}),{
's.1':{title:'Why the Bible starts with a good world that goes wrong',dek:'Genesis 1–11 introduces the basic tension the rest of the biblical story keeps answering: creation is called good, yet human violence, mistrust, and pride spread quickly.',body:['The opening chapters of Genesis move from a world repeatedly called good to a series of ruptures. Humans distrust God in the garden. A brother kills a brother. Violence fills the earth. After the flood, Babel becomes another picture of human ambition and fragmentation. The stories differ, but together they establish a widening problem rather than a single isolated mistake.','That widening pattern matters because the Bible does not begin with Israel, Moses, or Jesus. It begins with humanity and the world. When Abraham appears in Genesis 12, the story narrows to one family, but the problem that family is called into remains universal. The promise to Abraham will eventually include blessing reaching beyond his descendants.','Read these chapters as the opening movement of a much longer story. They are not a complete scientific account of origins, and the different scenes should not be flattened into one moral slogan. Their narrative job is to establish goodness, creaturely dependence, human agency, escalating disorder, judgment, preservation, and the need for a different future.'],plain:'The Bible starts wide: a good creation becomes a damaged human world. Everything that follows is partly about what God does with that problem.',visual:{type:'story-arc',title:'Genesis 1–11: the problem widens',beats:[{title:'Creation','books':'A world called good'},{title:'Garden','books':'Trust is ruptured'},{title:'Cain and Abel','books':'Violence enters family life'},{title:'Flood','books':'Violence fills the earth'},{title:'Babel','books':'Humanity fractures and scatters'}]},challenge:{kind:'sequence-path',title:'Rebuild the opening movement',prompt:'Put the Genesis 1–11 movements in narrative order.',items:['Creation is called good.','Trust ruptures in the garden.','Cain kills Abel.','The flood answers a world filled with violence.','Babel ends with scattering.'],answer:[0,1,2,3,4],hints:['Start with creation and end with the scattering at Babel.','The first murder comes before the flood.'],why:'Genesis 1–11 deliberately moves from creation to increasingly social forms of rupture.'}},
's.2':{title:'Why the story suddenly narrows to one family',dek:'Genesis 12–50 follows Abraham, Isaac, Jacob, and Joseph because a promise to one family is framed as part of God’s answer to a much wider human problem.',body:['After eleven chapters about humanity as a whole, Genesis 12 focuses on Abram. God promises land, descendants, and blessing—and says that blessing is meant to reach beyond Abram’s immediate household. The story becomes particular without becoming merely private.','The family that carries the promise is not idealized. Abraham acts fearfully. Jacob deceives and is deceived. Joseph’s brothers sell him. The narrative lets promise and dysfunction coexist. That is important: election in Genesis is not a certificate that every action of the chosen family is morally exemplary.','By the end of Genesis the family is in Egypt, where Joseph’s rise has preserved them during famine. That ending sets up Exodus. A family has become a people, and the place that once provided refuge will become the setting of slavery.'],plain:'The story zooms in on Abraham’s family because their promise is supposed to become good news beyond themselves—and because their move to Egypt sets up Exodus.',visual:{type:'relationship',title:'Four generations carry the promise',nodes:[{title:'Abraham',role:'Called; receives the promise'},{title:'Isaac',role:'Promise continues through the next generation'},{title:'Jacob / Israel',role:'Twelve sons become the family lines of Israel'},{title:'Joseph',role:'Family survives famine in Egypt'}],note:'The family line carries the story; it does not make every family action exemplary.'},challenge:{kind:'sequence-path',title:'Follow the family line',prompt:'Put the four generations in order.',items:['Abraham','Isaac','Jacob','Joseph'],answer:[0,1,2,3],hints:['Isaac is Abraham’s son; Jacob is Isaac’s son.'],why:'Abraham → Isaac → Jacob → Joseph is the family sequence that carries Genesis toward Egypt.'}},
's.3':{title:'From rescue to covenant: why Exodus does not end at the Red Sea',dek:'The Exodus story moves from liberation out of slavery to covenant, law, worship, and a long wilderness formation.',body:['Exodus begins with an enslaved people and a ruler who fears their growth. God hears their suffering, calls Moses, confronts Pharaoh, and brings the people out. That liberation is the first major movement—but it is not the end of the book’s purpose.','At Sinai, a rescued people becomes a covenant community. Law follows liberation rather than preceding it in the narrative. Leviticus asks how a community lives with holiness and worship. Numbers follows the difficult wilderness years. Deuteronomy retells and renews the covenant as the next generation stands near the land.','The sequence matters because it prevents a caricature of Torah as a random rulebook dropped on people to make them worthy of rescue. In the story, rescue comes first; covenant life follows. The wilderness then reveals how difficult freedom can be when habits of fear and distrust remain.'],plain:'Exodus is rescue, Sinai is relationship, Torah is covenant life, and the wilderness is where a freed people learns—often badly—how to live as that people.',visual:{type:'flow',title:'Rescue becomes a way of life',nodes:[{title:'Slavery',text:'A people under Pharaoh’s power'},{title:'Liberation',text:'Exodus from Egypt'},{title:'Covenant',text:'Sinai: a committed relationship'},{title:'Instruction',text:'Torah shapes worship and community'},{title:'Wilderness',text:'Trust and formation are tested'}]},challenge:{kind:'timeline-sort',title:'Rebuild the movement from Egypt to the land',prompt:'Place these major movements in story order.',items:['Liberation from Egypt','Covenant at Sinai','Wilderness testing','Deuteronomy’s covenant renewal near the land'],answer:[0,1,2,3],hints:['Sinai comes after the departure from Egypt.','Deuteronomy stands at the end of the wilderness period.'],why:'The Pentateuch moves from rescue to covenant to wilderness formation and renewed instruction.'}},
's.4':{title:'Land gained, then a cycle of chaos',dek:'Joshua and Judges belong together because settlement in the land is followed by repeated collapse, rescue, and renewed collapse.',body:['Joshua tells the story of entering and dividing the land. Judges opens after Joshua’s generation and presents a much less stable picture. Instead of one smooth national rise, Israel repeatedly falls into crisis.','The recurring pattern in Judges is easy to recognize: the people turn away, oppression follows, they cry out, a deliverer rises, and there is temporary relief. But the cycles do not simply repeat at the same level. The book grows darker. The closing stories show social and moral breakdown without presenting every narrated action as acceptable.','Ruth sits beside this chaotic period and offers a much smaller story of loyalty, vulnerability, provision, and family continuity. Together the books complicate any idea that possessing land automatically produces faithful community.'],plain:'Joshua gets Israel into the land. Judges shows that living there faithfully is another problem entirely.',visual:{type:'flow',title:'The Judges cycle',nodes:[{title:'Turning away',text:'Covenant trust erodes'},{title:'Crisis',text:'Oppression or conflict follows'},{title:'Cry for help',text:'The people appeal for rescue'},{title:'Deliverer',text:'A judge brings temporary relief'},{title:'Repeat—worse',text:'The cycle returns and darkens'}]},challenge:{kind:'sequence-path',title:'Recognize the Judges cycle',prompt:'Arrange the recurring pattern.',items:['The people turn away.','A crisis/oppressor dominates.','The people cry out.','A judge/deliverer brings relief.','The cycle begins again.'],answer:[0,1,2,3,4],hints:['The cry for help comes after the crisis.'],why:'Judges uses a repeated cycle to show instability and worsening communal breakdown.'}},
's.5':{title:'Why the monarchy feels like a high point—and a warning',dek:'Saul, David, and Solomon create Israel’s united monarchy, but the story never lets royal success erase royal failure.',body:['The books of Samuel begin with Israel asking for a king. The request is not treated simply as a triumph. Samuel warns that kings can take sons, daughters, fields, labor, and wealth. Saul becomes the first king and then loses legitimacy.','David unifies the kingdom, establishes Jerusalem, and receives a promise connected with his dynasty. He is also responsible for serious abuse of power. The narrative preserves both realities. Solomon builds the temple and becomes associated with wisdom and international prestige, while his accumulation of wealth, political marriages, and forced labor anticipate the kingdom’s fracture.','This is why the monarchy is both a high-water mark and the beginning of the next crisis. Jerusalem, temple, Davidic hope, and royal language become crucial themes—but biblical kingship is never a simple lesson that stronger centralized power solves human failure.'],plain:'Israel reaches political strength under David and Solomon, but the same stories expose what kings can corrupt. The peak already contains the seeds of division.',visual:{type:'relationship',title:'The united monarchy',nodes:[{title:'Saul',role:'First king; rise and failure'},{title:'David',role:'Jerusalem, dynasty promise, serious moral failure'},{title:'Solomon',role:'Temple, wisdom, wealth, compromise'}],note:'The monarchy creates lasting hopes while demonstrating the dangers of concentrated power.'},challenge:{kind:'match-board',title:'Match the king to the turning point',prompt:'Connect each king with the description that best fits his place in the story.',items:['Saul','David','Solomon'],options:['Builds the temple and presides over the kingdom’s wealthy peak','First king whose rule unravels','Establishes Jerusalem and receives a dynasty promise'],answer:[1,2,0],hints:['The temple is associated with Solomon.','Saul comes before David.'],why:'Saul, David, and Solomon carry distinct turning points in the united monarchy.'}},
's.6':{title:'One kingdom becomes two—and prophets refuse to stay quiet',dek:'After Solomon, Israel divides into northern Israel and southern Judah. Prophets then speak into centuries of political, religious, and economic failure.',body:['The united kingdom fractures after Solomon. The northern kingdom is usually called Israel; the southern kingdom is Judah. From this point, “Israel” can mean the whole covenant people in some contexts or specifically the northern kingdom in others, so careful reading matters.','Prophets do more than predict distant events. They address rulers, worship, exploitation, violence, alliances, idolatry, and covenant unfaithfulness in real historical settings. Amos speaks against injustice. Hosea uses marriage imagery. Isaiah and Micah address Judah. Jeremiah lives into Jerusalem’s final crisis.','The northern kingdom falls to Assyria in 722 BC. Judah survives longer but eventually falls to Babylon, with Jerusalem and the temple destroyed in 586 BC. Those two dates orient a large amount of prophetic literature.'],plain:'After Solomon, there are two kingdoms. Prophets spend centuries telling both that worship without faithfulness and justice will not save them from consequences.',visual:{type:'timeline',title:'Two kingdoms moving toward collapse',points:[{date:'c. 930 BC',title:'Kingdom divides',note:'Israel north / Judah south'},{date:'8th century BC',title:'Amos, Hosea, Isaiah, Micah',note:'Prophetic warning intensifies'},{date:'722 BC',title:'Assyria defeats northern Israel',note:'Northern kingdom falls'},{date:'586 BC',title:'Babylon destroys Jerusalem',note:'Judah falls'}]},challenge:{kind:'evidence-lab',title:'Keep the kingdoms straight',prompt:'Sort the claims.',lanes:['Supported','Possible','Overreach'],items:['The northern kingdom is commonly called Israel.','Judah survives after the northern kingdom falls.','Prophets only predict events thousands of years in the future.','722 BC and 586 BC are useful historical anchors.'],answer:[[0,0],[1,0],[2,2],[3,0]],hints:['Read prophetic books as speeches into historical crises as well as sources of hope.'],why:'The divided-monarchy period requires distinguishing Israel/Judah and locating prophets around key historical collapses.'}},
's.7':{title:'586 BC: the trauma that changes the shape of the Old Testament story',dek:'Babylon’s destruction of Jerusalem and the temple is more than another military defeat; exile becomes a theological and cultural crisis.',body:['Babylon’s campaigns against Judah culminate in Jerusalem’s destruction in 586 BC. The temple is burned, the monarchy ends, and many people are deported. Others remain in the land or flee elsewhere. “Exile” is therefore a major displacement, not the literal removal of every Judean person.','Lamentations gives poetic voice to devastation. Ezekiel speaks among deportees. Parts of Jeremiah wrestle with judgment, survival, and future hope. Daniel is set around imperial courts and faithfulness under foreign rule. Later biblical writing repeatedly looks back to exile as a symbol of loss and estrangement.','The crisis forces hard questions: Can covenant identity survive without land, king, or temple? Has God abandoned the people? What does faithfulness look like under empire? The answers become central to later hopes of return, restoration, renewed covenant, and divine presence.'],plain:'586 BC is the hinge: Jerusalem and the temple fall, many are deported, and Israel’s faith has to reckon with life after national collapse.',visual:{type:'timeline',title:'The exile hinge',points:[{date:'605/597 BC',title:'Earlier Babylonian deportations',note:'Pressure builds before final destruction'},{date:'586 BC',title:'Jerusalem destroyed',note:'Temple burned; monarchy ends'},{date:'Exilic period',title:'Life under empire',note:'Lament, prophecy, adaptation, hope'},{date:'538 BC',title:'Persian return policy',note:'A new phase begins'}]},challenge:{kind:'context-lens',title:'Read exile as more than a date',prompt:'Choose the most responsible description for each dimension.',fields:['Event','Loss','Question','Hope'],options:[['Babylon destroys Jerusalem in 586 BC','Rome destroys Jerusalem in AD 70'],['Temple, monarchy, homeland security','Only one private religious ritual'],['Can identity and covenant survive displacement?','Which Bible translation should be printed?'],['Return, restoration, renewed presence','Pretend the destruction never occurred']],answer:[0,0,0,0],hints:['Think nationally, socially, and theologically.'],why:'Exile combines historical destruction with questions of identity, presence, judgment, and restoration.'}},
's.8':{title:'Return does not mean everything goes back to the way it was',dek:'Persian policy allows Judeans to return and rebuild, but the post-exilic story is one of partial restoration, not a simple reset.',body:['After Babylon falls to Persia, Cyrus permits displaced peoples—including Judeans—to return and restore sanctuaries. Biblical return narratives focus on rebuilding the temple, restoring communal practices, and later rebuilding Jerusalem’s walls.','Ezra and Nehemiah describe reform efforts and difficult community boundaries. Haggai and Zechariah speak into the rebuilding period. Malachi addresses a later community still wrestling with worship and faithfulness. The restored province is smaller and remains under imperial rule; the Davidic monarchy is not simply reestablished.','That incompleteness matters for the later biblical story. Return is real, but many hopes remain unresolved. By the time of Jesus, Jewish life includes Judea, Galilee, and a broad diaspora under new empires. The “four hundred years of silence” shorthand can describe the gap between Protestant Old and New Testaments, but it should not imply that Jewish history, literature, theology, or political struggle stopped.'],plain:'People return and rebuild, but restoration is partial. Jewish life continues dynamically between the Testaments even though the Protestant biblical shelf has a gap.',visual:{type:'flow',title:'Return is a process, not a reset button',nodes:[{title:'Persian permission',text:'Return and restoration become possible'},{title:'Temple rebuilt',text:'Worship center restored'},{title:'Walls and community',text:'Nehemiah/Ezra reforms'},{title:'Ongoing empire',text:'Political independence is limited'},{title:'Unresolved hope',text:'Expectations continue into later Jewish history'}]},challenge:{kind:'evidence-lab',title:'Calibrate “return”',prompt:'Sort the statements.',lanes:['Supported','Possible','Overreach'],items:['Persian rule creates space for return and rebuilding.','The post-exilic community still faces unresolved problems.','Nothing happens in Jewish history between Malachi and Matthew.','Return instantly restores the monarchy and every earlier condition.'],answer:[[0,0],[1,0],[2,2],[3,2]],hints:['A gap in one canon is not a gap in history.'],why:'The return period is genuine restoration under continuing imperial conditions, not a complete reset.'}},
's.9':{title:'Four Gospels, one Jesus, four deliberate portraits',dek:'Matthew, Mark, Luke, and John overlap heavily but are not four copies of the same biography.',body:['The four canonical Gospels tell the story of Jesus’ ministry, death, and resurrection, but each writer arranges and emphasizes material in distinctive ways. Mark moves quickly and highlights conflict and suffering. Matthew repeatedly connects Jesus with Israel’s Scriptures and teaching. Luke pairs Jesus’ story with Acts and emphasizes the widening reach of the good news. John uses long dialogues and distinctive signs and imagery.','Differences in selection and arrangement are not automatically contradictions. Ancient biography did not require four writers to include the same episodes in the same order or with identical wording. At the same time, differences should not be denied when they are actually present; comparison is part of responsible reading.','The Gospels are also Jewish texts about a Jewish Jesus and his earliest Jewish followers. Christian readings must resist turning disputes inside first-century Judaism into excuses for hostility toward Jewish people as a whole.'],plain:'The Gospels agree on the central story but tell it with different emphases. Read each one as an author making a case, not as a transcript generated from the others.',visual:{type:'compare',title:'Four Gospel portraits',columns:[{title:'Synoptic Gospels',items:['Matthew, Mark, Luke','Share many events and sayings','Can be compared closely for arrangement and emphasis']},{title:'John',items:['Distinctive signs and long dialogues','Different chronology/emphases in places','Still centers on Jesus’ identity, death, and resurrection']}],shared:['Jesus is Jewish','Public ministry','Conflict','Crucifixion','Resurrection proclamation']},challenge:{kind:'compare-board',title:'Compare without flattening',prompt:'Place each statement where it belongs.',lanes:['Responsible','Depends on the Gospel','Overreach'],items:['Each Gospel selects and arranges material purposefully.','Every Gospel uses exactly the same sequence and wording.','John often presents material differently from the Synoptics.','A difference in arrangement automatically proves fabrication.'],answer:[0,2,1,2],hints:['Difference and contradiction are not synonyms.'],why:'The four Gospels are related witnesses with distinct literary strategies and emphases.'}},
's.10':{title:'The Jesus movement spreads—and the Bible closes with creation renewed',dek:'Acts and the New Testament letters show communities learning to live the Jesus story; Revelation ends the canon with judgment, endurance, and a world made new.',body:['Acts begins in Jerusalem and tracks a widening witness through Judea, Samaria, and the broader Roman world. The story introduces conflicts over inclusion, leadership, resources, law, persecution, and mission. The Church does not emerge as a conflict-free institution; discernment happens inside real disputes.','The letters are not a second narrative sequel in chronological order. They are communications into particular communities: Corinth, Rome, Galatia, Philippi, scattered congregations, leaders, and others. They show what the proclamation of Jesus looks like when communities have to address worship, sex, money, status, suffering, leadership, ethics, and disagreement.','Revelation uses apocalyptic imagery to address faithfulness under pressure and to imagine judgment on oppressive power. Its final movement returns to imagery from Genesis: creation renewed, death defeated, God dwelling with humanity. The canon therefore ends not with escape from creation but with a vision of creation healed.'],plain:'Acts shows the movement spreading, letters show communities working out what that means, and Revelation closes with endurance and renewed creation.',visual:{type:'story-arc',title:'From Jerusalem to new creation',beats:[{title:'Jerusalem','books':'Acts begins'},{title:'Widening mission','books':'Judea · Samaria · nations'},{title:'Communities form','books':'Letters address real churches'},{title:'Pressure and endurance','books':'General letters / Revelation'},{title:'Renewed creation','books':'Revelation 21–22'}]},challenge:{kind:'sequence-path',title:'Rebuild the New Testament movement',prompt:'Put the broad movements in order.',items:['Jesus’ followers begin witnessing from Jerusalem.','The mission widens across regions and peoples.','Letters address the life and problems of Christian communities.','Revelation calls for faithful endurance and envisions judgment.','The canon closes with renewed-creation imagery.'],answer:[0,1,2,3,4],hints:['Start with Acts and end with Revelation 21–22.'],why:'The final biblical movement runs from expanding witness through community formation to apocalyptic endurance and renewed creation.'}}
});
